<Base title={title} description={description} updated="2026-11-22">
  <h1>How to get an apostille in Texas</h1>
  <p class="lede">The Texas Secretary of State, Authentications Unit, issues a “universal Apostille.” Officially, that certificate operates as an apostille for countries that are party to the Apostille Convention and as an authentication for countries that are not.</p>

  <h2>At a glance</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Item</th><th>What the official page says</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Issuing office</td><td>Office of the Texas Secretary of State, Authentications Unit, 400 West 15th Street, Austin, TX 78701<Src href="https://www.sos.texas.gov/statdoc/request.shtml" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fee</td><td>$15.00 per universal apostille, not per page. For an adoption, $10.00 per apostille, and total fees may not exceed $100.00 for the adoption of each child. Adoption requests use Form 2103.<Src href="https://www.sos.state.tx.us/authfaqs.shtml" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mail</td><td>Yes, first come, first served. Include Form 2102, payment, and a prepaid return envelope. Credit-card payment is not accepted through the mail.<Src href="https://www.sos.texas.gov/statdoc/forms/2102new.pdf" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mailing address</td><td>Authentications Unit, P.O. Box 13550, Austin, TX 78711-3550, as printed on official Form 2102. The physical address for walk-in and appointment service is 400 West 15th St., Austin, TX 78701.<Src href="https://www.sos.texas.gov/statdoc/forms/2102new.pdf" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>In person</td><td>Same-day service for a maximum of 10 documents per person, company, or transaction. Appointments are required Tuesday through Thursday (one appointment per person or company per day). Walk-in service is Monday and Friday only, no appointment. Groups should be limited to no more than two people. The office will not pull a mailed request for walk-in service.<Src href="https://www.sos.texas.gov/statdoc/request.shtml" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drop-off</td><td>More than 10 documents may be dropped off Monday through Friday for a three-day / 72-hour processing turnaround. Requests must be registered in the office’s QR drop-box system and must include the bulk drop-off form.<Src href="https://www.sos.texas.gov/statdoc/request.shtml" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Processing as published</td><td>In person: same day. Drop-off: within 72 hours / three days. Mail: “can take up to two weeks from the day of receipt”; “actual processing times may vary depending on volume.” The office also says “Please allow at least two weeks from the date we receive your documents for processing.”<Src href="https://www.sos.texas.gov/statdoc/request.shtml" /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Hours and payment</h2>
  <p>The walk-in office is listed at 400 West 15th Street, Austin, TX 78701, 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., Monday through Friday. The request page warns that in-person wait times may exceed an hour, and that the office was closed Wednesday, November 25, through Friday, November 27, 2026, for Thanksgiving — confirm current closures before you go.<Src href="https://www.sos.texas.gov/statdoc/request.shtml" /></p>
  <p>Walk-in payment may be by check, money order, credit or debit card, or cash with exact change. Mailed requests may be paid by check or money order drawn on a U.S. bank and payable to the Office of the Texas Secretary of State, or by funding an SOS client account. The FAQ says checks and money orders should be in favor of the “Secretary of State of Texas.” Personal checks or money orders missing the amount, the requestor’s complete address, or the payee’s signature will not be accepted.<Src href="https://www.sos.texas.gov/statdoc/request.shtml" /><Src href="https://www.sos.state.tx.us/authfaqs.shtml" /></p>

  <h2>Which documents qualify</h2>
  <p>All documents must be issued from the state of Texas or properly notarized by a Texas notary public. You must submit the original Texas-issued document, a Texas-issued certified copy, or an original Texas-notarized document. The office does not apostille copies of certified or notarized records, and it does not apostille documents for use within the United States or its territories. Certified records must have been issued within the past five years; the official page says documents older than five years may not be verifiable and cannot be apostilled.<Src href="https://www.sos.texas.gov/statdoc/request.shtml" /></p>
  <p>You must indicate the destination country on Form 2102.<Src href="https://www.sos.texas.gov/statdoc/request.shtml" /></p>
  <p>If the destination country is not a party to the Apostille Convention, the official FAQ says you “likely need to get your universal Apostille validated by the Office of Authentications with the U.S. State Department.”<Src href="https://www.sos.state.tx.us/authfaqs.shtml" /></p>

  <h2>Remote online notarization</h2>
  <p>Texas will apostille an online (RON) notarization if you also present a notarization ledger (date and time signatures were applied, document description, and signer information) and a notarial certificate stating the document was notarized via “electronic or online notarization by two-way audio and visual communication.” The official page warns that some countries do not accept online notarized documents even after this office authenticates them.<Src href="https://www.sos.texas.gov/authinfo.shtml" /> See <a href="/online-notarization-for-apostille/">online notarization for apostille documents</a>.</p>

